course: everyday
lesson: 524
title: «Where's your dad?»: questions and cruel jokes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Kids ask about families all the time. Some ask because they're curious; some say things to hurt. On Father's Day a boy yells across the playground, «Oi Corey, where's your dad then? Did he do a runner?» («do a runner» = run off, leave without a word). Another kid adds, «No dad, massive L» («L» means a loss, a fail). A few laugh. Corey's face burns. He wants to punch him, but he walks off and tells Mr Hassan, who deals with it that afternoon. Jokes about someone's family are bullying, and the one being unkind is the joker, never the kid without a dad.
explain: Some words cut deep. A girl finds out Sienna-Rae is in foster care and says, «Your own mum didn't want you.» Sienna-Rae runs to the toilets and cries. It's cruel, and it isn't true: children go into care because parents can't cope or can't keep them safe, not because the child isn't wanted. Her teacher hears about it straight away and the girl's parents are called in. Minnie, who is adopted, gets told, «Your real mum gave you away.» She doesn't even blink: «My real mum is at home making my tea.» A kid sneers at Jamal, «Your dad's a jailbird!» (an old word for someone in prison). Kids laugh, and Jamal wants to cry. He tells his teacher the next day, and the school calls a charity that helps kids with a parent in prison.
explain: Blunt questions aren't always meant to hurt, but you still don't owe an answer. «Why you live with your grandma? Your parents dead or somethin'?» a kid asks Darius. «None of your business, bruh,» he says («bruh» = bro, mate). That's a fair answer. Kids keep asking Ffion why her parents split. «Dunno,» she says every time. She does know. Some things are private, and keeping them private isn't lying to anyone.
explain: Some teasing is about things a child can't help. Kids say Holly-Mae smells. Sometimes she does, because the washing machine has been broken for a month and there's no money to fix it. She feels ashamed. She tells her teacher, quietly, and the school washes her uniform in the school machine twice a week; nobody else knows. A kid says to Elijah, «That's weird, you can't have two dads.» «Well, I do. Deal with it.» Some families have two dads or two mums, and that's normal. A boy says something rude about Aziza's mum's headscarf. Aziza goes quiet. Mocking someone's religion or clothes is bullying, and her teacher makes sure it stops.
explain: At pick-up, Liam-Jay's mum is stumbling and slurring. A kid jeers, «Yer maw's steamin'!» («steaming» is Scots slang for drunk). Liam-Jay is mortified, which means so embarrassed he wants to vanish. It's not his fault, and he's not alone: he can tell a teacher, or call Childline for free on 0800 1111. Not every tease needs a teacher, though. A kid laughs at Kwesi's trainers: «Primark special!» (Primark is a cheap clothes shop). Kwesi grins. «Still faster than you, mate.» Everyone laughs, with him this time. Humour can stop small teasing; if it keeps going, or it's about your family, tell an adult.
fact: Some children ask about other families because they're curious; some say things to hurt.
fact: Jokes and cruel comments about someone's family are bullying.
fact: Walking away and telling a teacher is smarter than hitting back; schools must deal with bullying.
fact: Children go into care because parents can't cope or can't keep them safe, not because the child isn't wanted.
fact: Adoptive parents are real parents; a calm, proud answer can stop a cruel comment.
fact: A parent's mistakes, like being in prison, are not the child's fault or the child's shame.
fact: You don't owe anyone an answer about your family; «None of your business» or «Dunno» is allowed, and keeping something private isn't lying.
fact: Teasing about things a child can't help, like money or a broken washing machine, is cruel; schools can help quietly.
fact: Some families have two dads or two mums, and that is normal.
fact: Mocking someone's religion or clothes, like a headscarf, is bullying.
fact: A child whose parent drinks too much is not to blame; they can tell a teacher or call Childline for free on 0800 1111.
fact: Humour can stop small teasing, but if it keeps going or is about family, tell an adult.
fact: «Do a runner» means run off, «L» means a loss, «jailbird» is an old word for a prisoner, «bruh» means bro, «mardy» means sulky, «steaming» is Scots slang for drunk, and «mortified» means terribly embarrassed.
exercise: «Did he do a runner?» What does «do a runner» mean? | Run off, leave without a word | Go jogging every morning | Win a running race | Work as a pizza delivery driver
exercise: «No dad, massive L.» What does «L» mean? | A loss, a fail | A big laugh | Large size | A learner driver on the road
exercise: What did Corey do after the Father's Day joke? | Walked off and told Mr Hassan | Punched the boy right there in the playground | Laughed along so nobody would notice | Shouted a joke about the boy's mum back
exercise: Who is in the wrong in the Father's Day joke? | The joker | Corey, for not having his dad there that day | Corey's dad, for not coming to school | The kids who stood near Corey
exercise: «Your own mum didn't want you.» Is that true about children in care? | No, parents usually can't cope, it's not about the child | Yes, children only go into care if nobody wants them | Yes, unless the child is visited at least once a month | No, children in care all have parents who have died
exercise: What did Sienna-Rae's teacher do? | Dealt with it, and called the girl's parents in | Told Sienna-Rae to toughen up and ignore it | Said it was just a joke and not to make a fuss | Moved Sienna-Rae to another class for good
exercise: «Your real mum gave you away.» What did Minnie say? | «My real mum is at home making my tea» | «You're right, I'm not really wanted by anyone» | «My real mum is a princess in Spain» | Nothing, she ran to the toilets
exercise: What is a «jailbird»? | An old word for a prisoner | A bird that lives in a cage at the zoo | A police officer | A very fast runner
exercise: «Your parents dead or somethin'?» What did Darius say? | «None of your business, bruh» | His whole family story, in detail | «Yeah, they're dead,» which isn't true | He cried and went home early that day
exercise: Ffion knows why her parents split but says «Dunno». Is that okay? | Yes, some things are private | No, she must tell everyone | No, that's a horrible lie | Yes, but only to teachers
exercise: Why does Holly-Mae sometimes smell? | The washing machine is broken and there's no money to fix it | She refuses to have a bath because she thinks it's boring | Her family doesn't care about her at all, the kids say | She plays in the mud every day after school on purpose
exercise: How did the school help Holly-Mae? | Washed her uniform quietly | Told the whole class why | Sent her home every day | Bought her a new house
exercise: «You can't have two dads.» What's true? | Some families have two dads, and it's normal | Every child must have exactly one dad | Two dads is only allowed in films and on telly | Elijah must be making it up
exercise: A boy is rude about Aziza's mum's headscarf. What is it? | Bullying | Just a joke | A compliment | Fair comment
exercise: «Yer maw's steamin'!» What does «steaming» mean here? | Drunk | Very hot | Cooking | Furious
exercise: Liam-Jay's mum drinks too much. Whose fault is it? | Not his | His, for being naughty | His teacher's | His friends'
exercise: Who can Liam-Jay call for free? | Childline, on 0800 1111 | The police, to arrest his mum | The kid who teased him | Nobody, he has to cope alone
exercise: «Primark special!» What did Kwesi do? | Made a joke back, and everyone laughed with him | Took his trainers off and threw them at the kid | Asked his mum for expensive trainers that night | Cried and never wore those trainers again
test: «it's only banter, why's Corey being mardy». Is it only banter? | No, jokes about family are bullying | Yes, it's just a laugh and harmless | Yes, Corey can't take a joke | No, banter is only about football
test: What would most likely happen if Corey had punched the boy? | Corey would be in trouble too | The boy would say sorry at once | Mr Hassan would give Corey a prize | The teasing would stop forever
test: «she's in care cos her mum didn't want her, everyone says». What's true? | Children go into care when parents can't cope or keep them safe | It's true, because that is the only reason children go into care | It's true if lots of people at school are saying the same thing | Children go into care because they were too naughty at home
test: In a cartoon, the kid with a dad in prison is a villain too. What's more real? | A parent's mistakes are not the child's | Kids of prisoners are always trouble | It runs in families, like hair colour | Cartoons always get prison right
test: «why don't u just tell us why ur parents split, it's not a big deal». Must Ffion? | No, it's private, and «Dunno» is allowed | Yes, friends must share every secret | Yes, or she's a liar and a snake to her mates | No, but she must tell a teacher why
test: Holly-Mae's classmates: «she smells cos she's dirty». What's more likely and kinder to think? | Something at home is hard, like a broken washer | She's lazy and doesn't care what people think | Her family never washes anything at all, they say | She wants people to stay away from her
test: What would most likely happen if Liam-Jay told nobody about his mum's drinking? | He'd carry a big worry alone | His mum would stop on her own | The teasing would end all by itself in a day or two | Nothing at all would change
test: Kwesi's joke worked. Teasing goes on for weeks and gets nasty about his mum. What now? | Tell an adult; it's gone past a joke | Keep making jokes back, every day | Fight the kid after school | Stay home from school
test: ★★★ Kwesi ran 60 metres in 11 seconds. The boy who teased him took 13 seconds, and last week Kwesi took 12. How many seconds faster than the teaser was Kwesi today? | 2 | 1 | 13 | 24
test: ★★★ Sienna-Rae was upset on Monday. The head said she'd sort it «within three school days». There's an inset day (no school) on Wednesday. By which day at the latest? | Friday | Thursday | Wednesday | Monday
test: ★★★ Five kids laughed at Jamal. Next day two of them said sorry, and one more kid joined in laughing. How many are still laughing at him? | 4 | 3 | 5 | 6
test: Darius says «None of your business, bruh». Was that rude? | No, it's a fair answer to a nosy question | Yes, he must always answer every question | Yes, he should have said the whole story | No, but he must apologise straight after to the kid
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises remarks made concerning the family of another pupil? | Where intended to wound, they constitute bullying | They constitute harmless entertainment in every case | They are permissible provided that others laugh | They are the responsibility of the pupil concerned
exam: Identify the principal reason, as established in the lesson, for which children enter care. | Their parents are unable to cope or to keep them safe | Their parents have expressly declined to want them | They have conducted themselves poorly at school | They have themselves requested to leave home
exam: Given that a pupil is questioned concerning the separation of the parents, ascertain the pupil's obligation. | No obligation to disclose private matters exists | Full disclosure is required upon request | Disclosure is required to all classmates | A written account must be supplied
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a child whose parent consumes alcohol to excess. | The child is not at fault and may seek help from a teacher or Childline | The child bears responsibility and must conceal the matter at all times | The child ought to confront the parent publicly at the school gate before others | The child is prohibited from discussing the matter with any person
